From: Romeu Mello <romeumello@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:51:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] virtio/vsock: preserve half-close semantics in the unix
 proxy

When the host side of a unix socket proxy shut down its write side,
recv() returned 0 and the proxy answered with a reset, so the guest
could never send its reply. Clients such as the Docker CLI half-close
every request stream (`docker exec`, piped stdin), and the response
was lost.

Translate that EOF into a send-only vsock shutdown, ordered after the
data already pushed, stop read polling and keep late credit updates
from restarting it, as the TSI stream proxy does since 3f76a106. Fatal
socket errors still reset the connection.

Shut down each direction of the host socket separately: on macOS,
shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) on a socket whose peer already shut down its write
side fails with ENOTCONN, so the host only saw EOF once the proxy was
reaped.

On macOS, set SO_NOSIGPIPE on accepted sockets too: the guest can now
keep writing after the host side is gone, and that write must fail
with EPIPE instead of killing the VMM with SIGPIPE.

Add an end-to-end regression covering both directions (host connecting
to a guest listener and the guest connecting out) with small,
multi-buffer and empty requests followed by host SHUT_WR.

